## 10. CI/CD and GitHub Rules

If editing CI/CD or GitHub workflow files, inspect:

- workflow triggers
- branches
- secrets used by workflow
- build/test commands
- deployment steps
- permissions
- cache behavior
- environment protection rules
- rollback behavior

Rules:

- Do not broaden workflow permissions unless necessary.
- Do not print secrets in CI logs.
- Do not deploy from unintended branches.
- Do not add production deployment triggers casually.
- Do not change CI commands without checking package scripts.
- Do not make CI pass by skipping important checks unless explicitly approved.

---

## 11. Deployment Safety

Before production-affecting changes, require:

- changed-file summary
- affected environment summary
- migration/deployment risk
- rollback path
- smoke test plan
- manual approval

Production-affecting actions include:

- deploying app
- deploying Edge Functions
- pushing migrations
- changing production env vars
- changing CI deployment triggers
- changing domain/redirect/rewrite config
- changing auth callback URLs
- changing storage bucket access
- changing RLS policies

Do not perform or recommend these casually.

---

## 12. Verification Rules

For deployment/env changes, verification must be concrete.

Relevant checks may include:

- typecheck
- lint
- build
- local dev boot
- env var presence check using redacted output
- local Edge Function serve test
- preview deployment smoke test
- production smoke test only after explicit deployment
- auth redirect test
- Supabase connection test
- Twilio send test in safe environment
- no-secrets-in-client-bundle check if relevant

Cursor must report which checks were run and which were not.

Do not claim production verification unless production was actually checked.

---

## 13. Final Response Requirements

After deployment/env-related edits, Cursor must report:

- files changed
- why each file changed
- env vars added/renamed/removed
- deployment config changed
- CI/CD changed
- package scripts changed
- Supabase deployment behavior changed
- Edge Function deployment behavior changed
- secrets touched or intentionally not touched
- tests/checks run
- manual verification still needed
- unresolved risks
- rollback notes

If no files were edited because inspection showed uncertainty, report exactly what is missing.

---

## 14. Hard Red Lines

Never:

- expose secrets to client code
- print raw secrets
- commit `.env` secrets
- invent env var names
- assume npm
- assume local equals production
- run raw Supabase deployment or mutation commands; for live/Forensic V2 Edge Function deployment, only the human operator may run `scripts/deploy-functions-forensic-v2-live.ps1`
- push migrations without explicit instruction
- broaden CI permissions casually
- add production deployment triggers casually
- hide build errors by weakening types broadly
- move privileged keys into frontend code
- log sensitive payloads
- perform destructive commands without approval
- claim production verification without evidence

When uncertain, stop and inspect.